INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bvvt000medanksxkjorn','cmuh9bvvs000kedan7muo1hk3','Letter Sounds We Know','Connect letters to the sounds they make.','Welcome to today''s Language Arts lesson for Kindergarten at Prosper Preparatory.

Today we will learn about **Letter Sounds We Know**. Connect letters to the sounds they make.

Sit somewhere quiet, gather a pencil and paper, and take your time. Ready? Let''s begin.

## Learning goals

- Letters stand for sounds we hear in words.
- Saying sounds slowly helps us blend.
- Pictures can help us remember letter sounds.

## Teach

### Idea 1

Letters stand for sounds we hear in words.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

### Idea 2

Saying sounds slowly helps us blend.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

### Idea 3

Pictures can help us remember letter sounds.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

## Try it with me

Think about **Letter Sounds We Know** in your own home or neighborhood. Talk with a grown-up about what you notice. Look for everyday examples of today''s idea around you.

Use crayons or a pencil to make a quick drawing that shows today''s idea. Label one part of your drawing. Then tell someone one new word you learned.

If you finish early, teach a stuffed animal or sibling the same idea in your own words. Teaching others helps your brain remember.

## Let''s try one together

The letter Mm says /m/, like in *mom* and *map*. Point to Mm and say /m/.

## Watch for this mistake

A frequent error in **Letter Sounds We Know** is treating a slogan or a one-word label as mastery. Instead, restate the definition, show the worked example from memory, then complete practice with labeled steps or cited evidence.

## Practice

Complete these on your own first.

1. What sound does Ss make?
2. Name something that starts with Bb.
3. Clap the sounds in *cat*. How many?

## Check your work

Compare your answers only after you finish.

1. /s/ as in sun
2. Examples: ball, book, bear
3. 3 sounds: /c/ /a/ /t/

## Stretch

Find three objects at home that start with the same letter.

Share your stretch work with a parent, teacher, or classmate if you can. Growth happens when we explain our thinking.

## You did it

Great work today. Rest your eyes, then mark this lesson complete when you are ready. Tomorrow we will build on what you learned.','• Letters stand for sounds we hear in words.
• Saying sounds slowly helps us blend.
• Pictures can help us remember letter sounds.',1,20,'section-1','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=a4lVH2YdQnA');
INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bvvx0010edanl48ie67m','cmuh9bvvs000kedan7muo1hk3','Rhyming Words','Hear and make rhymes.','Welcome to today''s Language Arts lesson for Kindergarten at Prosper Preparatory.

Today we will learn about **Rhyming Words**. Hear and make rhymes.

Sit somewhere quiet, gather a pencil and paper, and take your time. Ready? Let''s begin.

## Learning goals

- Rhyming words share the same ending sound.
- Songs and poems use rhyme.
- We can invent new rhymes for fun.

## Teach

### Idea 1

Rhyming words share the same ending sound.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

### Idea 2

Songs and poems use rhyme.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

### Idea 3

We can invent new rhymes for fun.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

## Try it with me

Think about **Rhyming Words** in your own home or neighborhood. Talk with a grown-up about what you notice. Look for everyday examples of today''s idea around you.

Use crayons or a pencil to make a quick drawing that shows today''s idea. Label one part of your drawing. Then tell someone one new word you learned.

If you finish early, teach a stuffed animal or sibling the same idea in your own words. Teaching others helps your brain remember.

## Let''s try one together

Cat / hat / bat rhyme. Dog / log rhyme. Say each pair aloud.

## Watch for this mistake

A frequent error in **Rhyming Words** is treating a slogan or a one-word label as mastery. Instead, restate the definition, show the worked example from memory, then complete practice with labeled steps or cited evidence.

## Practice

Complete these on your own first.

1. Which rhymes with *sun*: run or sit?
2. Name a rhyme for *bee*.
3. Do *cup* and *cap* rhyme?

## Check your work

Compare your answers only after you finish.

1. run
2. Examples: me, see, tree
3. No — endings differ

## Stretch

Make a silly rhyme about your favorite animal.

Share your stretch work with a parent, teacher, or classmate if you can. Growth happens when we explain our thinking.

## You did it

Great work today. Rest your eyes, then mark this lesson complete when you are ready. Tomorrow we will build on what you learned.','• Rhyming words share the same ending sound.
• Songs and poems use rhyme.
• We can invent new rhymes for fun.',2,20,'section-1','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=a4lVH2YdQnA');
INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bvw0001eedan9fevb6fq','cmuh9bvvs000kedan7muo1hk3','Story Time: Characters','Notice who is in a story.','Welcome to today''s Language Arts lesson for Kindergarten at Prosper Preparatory.

Today we will learn about **Story Time: Characters**. Notice who is in a story.

Sit somewhere quiet, gather a pencil and paper, and take your time. Ready? Let''s begin.

## Learning goals

- Characters are the people or animals in a story.
- We learn about characters by what they do and say.
- Pictures give clues about characters.

## Teach

### Idea 1

Characters are the people or animals in a story.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

### Idea 2

We learn about characters by what they do and say.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

### Idea 3

Pictures give clues about characters.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

## Try it with me

Think about **Story Time: Characters** in your own home or neighborhood. Talk with a grown-up about what you notice. Look for everyday examples of today''s idea around you.

Use crayons or a pencil to make a quick drawing that shows today''s idea. Label one part of your drawing. Then tell someone one new word you learned.

If you finish early, teach a stuffed animal or sibling the same idea in your own words. Teaching others helps your brain remember.

## Let''s try one together

In *The Three Bears*, Goldilocks and the bears are characters. Goldilocks tries the porridge.

## Watch for this mistake

A frequent error in **Story Time: Characters** is treating a slogan or a one-word label as mastery. Instead, restate the definition, show the worked example from memory, then complete practice with labeled steps or cited evidence.

## Practice

Complete these on your own first.

1. Who is a character in your favorite story?
2. How can pictures help?
3. True or false: The setting is a character.

## Check your work

Compare your answers only after you finish.

1. Any named person/animal from the story
2. They show looks, feelings, and actions
3. False — setting is where/when

## Stretch

Draw your favorite story character and tell one thing they did.

Share your stretch work with a parent, teacher, or classmate if you can. Growth happens when we explain our thinking.

## You did it

Great work today. Rest your eyes, then mark this lesson complete when you are ready. Tomorrow we will build on what you learned.','• Characters are the people or animals in a story.
• We learn about characters by what they do and say.
• Pictures give clues about characters.',3,20,'section-1','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=a4lVH2YdQnA');
INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bvw3001sedanwhqp5b9f','cmuh9bvvs000kedan7muo1hk3','Story Time: Setting','Tell where and when a story happens.','Welcome to today''s Language Arts lesson for Kindergarten at Prosper Preparatory.

Today we will learn about **Story Time: Setting**. Tell where and when a story happens.

Sit somewhere quiet, gather a pencil and paper, and take your time. Ready? Let''s begin.

## Learning goals

- Setting is the place and time of a story.
- Words and pictures show the setting.
- Settings can change in a story.

## Teach

### Idea 1

Setting is the place and time of a story.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

### Idea 2

Words and pictures show the setting.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

### Idea 3

Settings can change in a story.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

## Try it with me

Think about **Story Time: Setting** in your own home or neighborhood. Talk with a grown-up about what you notice. Look for everyday examples of today''s idea around you.

Use crayons or a pencil to make a quick drawing that shows today''s idea. Label one part of your drawing. Then tell someone one new word you learned.

If you finish early, teach a stuffed animal or sibling the same idea in your own words. Teaching others helps your brain remember.

## Let''s try one together

A story in a forest at night has a different setting than one at school in the morning.

## Watch for this mistake

A frequent error in **Story Time: Setting** is treating a slogan or a one-word label as mastery. Instead, restate the definition, show the worked example from memory, then complete practice with labeled steps or cited evidence.

## Practice

Complete these on your own first.

1. Where does a farm story happen?
2. Name a time word (day, night…).
3. Can a story have more than one setting?

## Check your work

Compare your answers only after you finish.

1. On a farm / countryside
2. Examples: morning, night, winter
3. Yes

## Stretch

Describe the setting of a book you heard today.

Share your stretch work with a parent, teacher, or classmate if you can. Growth happens when we explain our thinking.

## You did it

Great work today. Rest your eyes, then mark this lesson complete when you are ready. Tomorrow we will build on what you learned.','• Setting is the place and time of a story.
• Words and pictures show the setting.
• Settings can change in a story.',4,20,'section-2','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=a4lVH2YdQnA');
INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bvw60026edanoi5qg447','cmuh9bvvs000kedan7muo1hk3','Listening for Details','Listen carefully and remember important parts.','Welcome to today''s Language Arts lesson for Kindergarten at Prosper Preparatory.

Today we will learn about **Listening for Details**. Listen carefully and remember important parts.

Sit somewhere quiet, gather a pencil and paper, and take your time. Ready? Let''s begin.

## Learning goals

- Good listeners look and stay quiet while someone reads.
- Details are small important facts.
- We can retell details in order.

## Teach

### Idea 1

Good listeners look and stay quiet while someone reads.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

### Idea 2

Details are small important facts.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

### Idea 3

We can retell details in order.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

## Try it with me

Think about **Listening for Details** in your own home or neighborhood. Talk with a grown-up about what you notice. Look for everyday examples of today''s idea around you.

Use crayons or a pencil to make a quick drawing that shows today''s idea. Label one part of your drawing. Then tell someone one new word you learned.

If you finish early, teach a stuffed animal or sibling the same idea in your own words. Teaching others helps your brain remember.

## Let''s try one together

If a story says the puppy hid under the porch, that detail helps us picture the scene.

## Watch for this mistake

A frequent error in **Listening for Details** is treating a slogan or a one-word label as mastery. Instead, restate the definition, show the worked example from memory, then complete practice with labeled steps or cited evidence.

## Practice

Complete these on your own first.

1. Name one good listening habit.
2. What is a detail?
3. Retell: Who? What?

## Check your work

Compare your answers only after you finish.

1. Eyes on speaker / quiet body / think about words
2. A small important fact from the story
3. Answers vary — should include character + action

## Stretch

Listen to a short paragraph and draw one detail you heard.

Share your stretch work with a parent, teacher, or classmate if you can. Growth happens when we explain our thinking.

## You did it

Great work today. Rest your eyes, then mark this lesson complete when you are ready. Tomorrow we will build on what you learned.','• Good listeners look and stay quiet while someone reads.
• Details are small important facts.
• We can retell details in order.',5,20,'section-2','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=a4lVH2YdQnA');
INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bvwa002kedanz970ddau','cmuh9bvvs000kedan7muo1hk3','Writing Our Names','Write letters of our names carefully.','Welcome to today''s Language Arts lesson for Kindergarten at Prosper Preparatory.

Today we will learn about **Writing Our Names**. Write letters of our names carefully.

Sit somewhere quiet, gather a pencil and paper, and take your time. Ready? Let''s begin.

## Learning goals

- Our names begin with a capital letter.
- We write left to right.
- Practice makes our writing clearer.

## Teach

### Idea 1

Our names begin with a capital letter.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

### Idea 2

We write left to right.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

### Idea 3

Practice makes our writing clearer.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

## Try it with me

Think about **Writing Our Names** in your own home or neighborhood. Talk with a grown-up about what you notice. Look for everyday examples of today''s idea around you.

Use crayons or a pencil to make a quick drawing that shows today''s idea. Label one part of your drawing. Then tell someone one new word you learned.

If you finish early, teach a stuffed animal or sibling the same idea in your own words. Teaching others helps your brain remember.

## Let''s try one together

Sam starts with capital S, then a, m. Trace slowly on lined paper.

## Watch for this mistake

A frequent error in **Writing Our Names** is treating a slogan or a one-word label as mastery. Instead, restate the definition, show the worked example from memory, then complete practice with labeled steps or cited evidence.

## Practice

Complete these on your own first.

1. What kind of letter starts a name?
2. Which way do we write?
3. Write your first name once.

## Check your work

Compare your answers only after you finish.

1. A capital letter
2. Left to right
3. Student produces name

## Stretch

Write your name three times, each neater than the last.

Share your stretch work with a parent, teacher, or classmate if you can. Growth happens when we explain our thinking.

## You did it

Great work today. Rest your eyes, then mark this lesson complete when you are ready. Tomorrow we will build on what you learned.','• Our names begin with a capital letter.
• We write left to right.
• Practice makes our writing clearer.',6,20,'section-2','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=a4lVH2YdQnA');
INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bvwd002yedanuf2tddhj','cmuh9bvvs000kedan7muo1hk3','Asking Questions About Books','Ask who, what, where, why.','Welcome to today''s Language Arts lesson for Kindergarten at Prosper Preparatory.

Today we will learn about **Asking Questions About Books**. Ask who, what, where, why.

Sit somewhere quiet, gather a pencil and paper, and take your time. Ready? Let''s begin.

## Learning goals

- Questions help us understand stories.
- Who/what/where/why are useful question words.
- We can ask questions before and after reading.

## Teach

### Idea 1

Questions help us understand stories.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

### Idea 2

Who/what/where/why are useful question words.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

### Idea 3

We can ask questions before and after reading.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

## Try it with me

Think about **Asking Questions About Books** in your own home or neighborhood. Talk with a grown-up about what you notice. Look for everyday examples of today''s idea around you.

Use crayons or a pencil to make a quick drawing that shows today''s idea. Label one part of your drawing. Then tell someone one new word you learned.

If you finish early, teach a stuffed animal or sibling the same idea in your own words. Teaching others helps your brain remember.

## Let''s try one together

Before reading: What do I see on the cover? After: Why did the character feel sad?

## Watch for this mistake

A frequent error in **Asking Questions About Books** is treating a slogan or a one-word label as mastery. Instead, restate the definition, show the worked example from memory, then complete practice with labeled steps or cited evidence.

## Practice

Complete these on your own first.

1. Name three question words.
2. Ask a *where* question about a park story.
3. Why ask questions?

## Check your work

Compare your answers only after you finish.

1. who, what, where, why, when, how
2. Where did they play?
3. To understand better

## Stretch

Ask two questions about tonight''s bedtime story.

Share your stretch work with a parent, teacher, or classmate if you can. Growth happens when we explain our thinking.

## You did it

Great work today. Rest your eyes, then mark this lesson complete when you are ready. Tomorrow we will build on what you learned.','• Questions help us understand stories.
• Who/what/where/why are useful question words.
• We can ask questions before and after reading.',7,20,'section-3','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=a4lVH2YdQnA');
INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bvwh003cedanxu96fkri','cmuh9bvvs000kedan7muo1hk3','Opposites and Describing Words','Use words that describe and opposite pairs.','Welcome to today''s Language Arts lesson for Kindergarten at Prosper Preparatory.

Today we will learn about **Opposites and Describing Words**. Use words that describe and opposite pairs.

Sit somewhere quiet, gather a pencil and paper, and take your time. Ready? Let''s begin.

## Learning goals

- Describing words tell how things look or feel.
- Opposites are words with opposite meanings.
- Good writers choose clear describing words.

## Teach

### Idea 1

Describing words tell how things look or feel.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

### Idea 2

Opposites are words with opposite meanings.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

### Idea 3

Good writers choose clear describing words.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

## Try it with me

Think about **Opposites and Describing Words** in your own home or neighborhood. Talk with a grown-up about what you notice. Look for everyday examples of today''s idea around you.

Use crayons or a pencil to make a quick drawing that shows today''s idea. Label one part of your drawing. Then tell someone one new word you learned.

If you finish early, teach a stuffed animal or sibling the same idea in your own words. Teaching others helps your brain remember.

## Let''s try one together

Big / little are opposites. Soft, red, and loud are describing words.

## Watch for this mistake

A frequent error in **Opposites and Describing Words** is treating a slogan or a one-word label as mastery. Instead, restate the definition, show the worked example from memory, then complete practice with labeled steps or cited evidence.

## Practice

Complete these on your own first.

1. What is the opposite of *hot*?
2. Name a describing word for a kitten.
3. Are *up* and *down* opposites?

## Check your work

Compare your answers only after you finish.

1. cold
2. soft, small, fluffy…
3. Yes

## Stretch

List five describing words for Texas weather.

Share your stretch work with a parent, teacher, or classmate if you can. Growth happens when we explain our thinking.

## You did it

Great work today. Rest your eyes, then mark this lesson complete when you are ready. Tomorrow we will build on what you learned.','• Describing words tell how things look or feel.
• Opposites are words with opposite meanings.
• Good writers choose clear describing words.',8,20,'section-3','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=a4lVH2YdQnA');
INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bvwk003qedan4pxnp173','cmuh9bvvs000kedan7muo1hk3','Sharing Our Ideas','Speak in complete thoughts about books.','Welcome to today''s Language Arts lesson for Kindergarten at Prosper Preparatory.

Today we will learn about **Sharing Our Ideas**. Speak in complete thoughts about books.

Sit somewhere quiet, gather a pencil and paper, and take your time. Ready? Let''s begin.

## Learning goals

- We take turns when we share.
- We use full sentences.
- We listen to friends'' ideas.

## Teach

### Idea 1

We take turns when we share.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

### Idea 2

We use full sentences.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

### Idea 3

We listen to friends'' ideas.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

## Try it with me

Think about **Sharing Our Ideas** in your own home or neighborhood. Talk with a grown-up about what you notice. Look for everyday examples of today''s idea around you.

Use crayons or a pencil to make a quick drawing that shows today''s idea. Label one part of your drawing. Then tell someone one new word you learned.

If you finish early, teach a stuffed animal or sibling the same idea in your own words. Teaching others helps your brain remember.

## Let''s try one together

Instead of saying "dog," say "The dog ran to the barn."

## Watch for this mistake

A frequent error in **Sharing Our Ideas** is treating a slogan or a one-word label as mastery. Instead, restate the definition, show the worked example from memory, then complete practice with labeled steps or cited evidence.

## Practice

Complete these on your own first.

1. Finish: The bird ___ .
2. Why take turns?
3. Say one sentence about school.

## Check your work

Compare your answers only after you finish.

1. Any complete idea, e.g. flew away
2. So everyone can share and listen
3. Complete sentence from student

## Stretch

Tell a partner three sentences about your day.

Share your stretch work with a parent, teacher, or classmate if you can. Growth happens when we explain our thinking.

## You did it

Great work today. Rest your eyes, then mark this lesson complete when you are ready. Tomorrow we will build on what you learned.','• We take turns when we share.
• We use full sentences.
• We listen to friends'' ideas.',9,20,'section-3','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=a4lVH2YdQnA');
INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bvx7005zedank6ur00r9','cmuh9bvx6005xedaniluy5yld','Counting to 20','Learn Counting to 20 through definitions, a worked example, guided practice, and a quick error check for Kindergarten Mathematics.','Welcome to today''s Mathematics lesson for Kindergarten at Prosper Preparatory.

Today we will learn about **Counting to 20**. Learn Counting to 20 through definitions, a worked example, guided practice, and a quick error check for Kindergarten Mathematics.

Sit somewhere quiet, gather a pencil and paper, and take your time. Ready? Let''s begin.

## Learning goals

- Today we learn about Counting to 20.
- Good learners go slowly: point, think, then answer.
- When you finish, tell someone one new thing you can do because of today''s lesson.

## Teach

### Idea 1

Today we learn about Counting to 20. We will look carefully, say ideas out loud, and try a small example together.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

### Idea 2

Good learners go slowly: point, think, then answer. If you are stuck, look back at the example.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

### Idea 3

When you finish, tell someone one new thing you can do because of today''s lesson.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

## Try it with me

Think about **Counting to 20** in your own home or neighborhood. Talk with a grown-up about what you notice. Look for everyday examples of today''s idea around you.

Use crayons or a pencil to make a quick drawing that shows today''s idea. Label one part of your drawing. Then tell someone one new word you learned.

If you finish early, teach a stuffed animal or sibling the same idea in your own words. Teaching others helps your brain remember.

## Let''s try one together

Let''s try one together for Counting to 20. First we look. Next we say what we notice. Then we try a tiny practice item and check it.

## Watch for this mistake

A frequent error in **Counting to 20** is treating a slogan or a one-word label as mastery. Instead, restate the definition, show the worked example from memory, then complete practice with labeled steps or cited evidence.

