INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bw4h00ycedanhn5v480o','cmuh9bw4400wqedanspkymmlv','Push and Pull Forces','Explain push and pull forces with models, vocabulary, and evidence from observations or diagrams.','Welcome to today''s Science lesson for Grade 1 at Prosper Preparatory.

Today we will learn about **Push and Pull Forces**. Explain push and pull forces with models, vocabulary, and evidence from observations or diagrams.

Sit somewhere quiet, gather a pencil and paper, and take your time. Ready? Let''s begin.

## Learning goals

- Science explains patterns with models.
- Distinguish observation (what you see/measure) from inference (what you conclude).
- Link structure to function: why a part''s shape or arrangement enables what it does (cells, molecules, Earth systems, forces).

## Teach

### Idea 1

Science explains patterns with models. For Push and Pull Forces, name the key parts/processes and how they interact.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

### Idea 2

Distinguish observation (what you see/measure) from inference (what you conclude). Controlled comparisons improve investigations.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

### Idea 3

Link structure to function: why a part''s shape or arrangement enables what it does (cells, molecules, Earth systems, forces).

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

## Try it with me

Think about **Push and Pull Forces** in your own home or neighborhood. Talk with a grown-up about what you notice. Look for everyday examples of today''s idea around you.

Use crayons or a pencil to make a quick drawing that shows today''s idea. Label one part of your drawing. Then tell someone one new word you learned.

If you finish early, teach a stuffed animal or sibling the same idea in your own words. Teaching others helps your brain remember.

## Let''s try one together

Forces can change motion. Inertia (1st law): objects keep velocity unless a net force acts. F = ma (2nd). Action–reaction pairs (3rd) are equal and opposite on different objects.

## Watch for this mistake

A frequent error in **Push and Pull Forces** is treating a slogan or a one-word label as mastery. Instead, restate the definition, show the worked example from memory, then complete practice with labeled steps or cited evidence.

## Practice

Complete these on your own first.

1. Define one key term from today’s science focus in your own words.
2. Give one observation and one inference related to this topic.
3. Ask one testable question about push and pull forces.

## Check your work

Compare your answers only after you finish.

1. Must match the lesson vocabulary (e.g., cell, evaporation, force) with a correct student paraphrase.
2. Observation = measurable/sensed; inference = conclusion based on evidence.
3. Question should be answerable with observation or experiment, not only opinion.

## Stretch

Draw and label a diagram for today''s topic. Add a caption explaining cause–effect in 3–4 sentences.

Share your stretch work with a parent, teacher, or classmate if you can. Growth happens when we explain our thinking.

## You did it

Great work today. Rest your eyes, then mark this lesson complete when you are ready. Tomorrow we will build on what you learned.','• Science explains patterns with models. For Push and Pull Forces, name the key parts/processes and how they interact.
• Distinguish observation (what you see/measure) from inference (what you conclude). Controlled comparisons improve investigations.
• Link structure to function: why a part''s shape or arrangement enables what it does (cells, molecules, Earth systems, forces).',5,20,'section-2','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kKKM8Y-u7ds');
