INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bwaw01nledandqvagtv1','cmuh9bwav01njedan66x8io9w','Our Classroom Community Rules','Analyze our classroom community rules with maps, timelines, sourcing, and civic vocabulary.','Welcome to today''s Social Studies lesson for Grade 2 at Prosper Preparatory.

Today we will learn about **Our Classroom Community Rules**. Analyze our classroom community rules with maps, timelines, sourcing, and civic vocabulary.

Sit somewhere quiet, gather a pencil and paper, and take your time. Ready? Let''s begin.

## Learning goals

- Ask who / what / when / where / why.
- Sources have authors, audiences, and purposes.
- Civic ideas (rights, responsibilities, constitutions, markets) connect past events to present participation.

## Teach

### Idea 1

Ask who / what / when / where / why. Geography and resources shape settlement, conflict, and culture.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

### Idea 2

Sources have authors, audiences, and purposes. Sourcing and corroboration reduce being misled by a single account.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

### Idea 3

Civic ideas (rights, responsibilities, constitutions, markets) connect past events to present participation.

Look at the words carefully. Say them out loud. Draw a small picture if it helps you remember.

## Try it with me

Think about **Our Classroom Community Rules** in your own home or neighborhood. Talk with a grown-up about what you notice. Look for everyday examples of today''s idea around you.

Use crayons or a pencil to make a quick drawing that shows today''s idea. Label one part of your drawing. Then tell someone one new word you learned.

If you finish early, teach a stuffed animal or sibling the same idea in your own words. Teaching others helps your brain remember.

## Let''s try one together

When reading a historical account, ask: Who wrote this? What was their point of view? What other source could confirm or challenge it?

## Watch for this mistake

A frequent error in **Our Classroom Community Rules** is treating a slogan or a one-word label as mastery. Instead, restate the definition, show the worked example from memory, then complete practice with labeled steps or cited evidence.

## Practice

Complete these on your own first.

1. Name one fact and one opinion related to today’s topic.
2. Why does sourcing a document matter?
3. Connect this topic to life in Texas or the U.S. today in one sentence.

## Check your work

Compare your answers only after you finish.

1. Fact = verifiable; opinion = belief/value. Examples will vary but must be correctly typed.
2. Author, date, and purpose affect reliability and help you detect bias.
3. Any reasonable civic, geographic, economic, or cultural connection.

## Stretch

Create a mini timeline (4 events) or a sourced paragraph with a thesis and two pieces of evidence.

Share your stretch work with a parent, teacher, or classmate if you can. Growth happens when we explain our thinking.

## You did it

Great work today. Rest your eyes, then mark this lesson complete when you are ready. Tomorrow we will build on what you learned.','• Ask who / what / when / where / why. Geography and resources shape settlement, conflict, and culture.
• Sources have authors, audiences, and purposes. Sourcing and corroboration reduce being misled by a single account.
• Civic ideas (rights, responsibilities, constitutions, markets) connect past events to present participation.',1,20,'section-1','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=24Y8v9xGd0Y');
