## Learning goals

- Context Clues for Vocabulary means you can explain the idea in your own words and show it with an example.
- Strong language arts work shows steps: define → try a model → solve → check.
- A common mistake is rushing to an answer without using the definition.

## Teach

### Key idea 1

Context Clues for Vocabulary means you can explain the idea in your own words and show it with an example.

Write one sentence in your notebook that restates this idea in your own words.

### Key idea 2

Strong language arts work shows steps: define → try a model → solve → check.

Write one sentence in your notebook that restates this idea in your own words.

### Key idea 3

A common mistake is rushing to an answer without using the definition. Slow down and label your work.

Write one sentence in your notebook that restates this idea in your own words.

## Guided practice notes

For Grade 3 Language Arts, strong habits matter as much as correct answers. Read each section twice. First, get the big idea. Second, hunt for details you can use in practice.

Texas classrooms often ask students to *show their work* and *explain their thinking*. Do the same here: write complete sentences when the question asks for explanation, and label diagrams clearly.

Common pitfall: rushing to the answer key. Cover the "Check your work" section until you have tried every item. Then correct in a different color so you can see growth.

## Worked example

Worked example for Context Clues for Vocabulary: (1) Restate the problem. (2) Identify the rule or definition that applies. (3) Carry out the steps with labels. (4) Check with an estimate, inverse operation, or evidence citation.

## Watch for this mistake

A frequent error in **Context Clues for Vocabulary** is treating a slogan or a one-word label as mastery. Instead, restate the definition, show the worked example from memory, then complete practice with labeled steps or cited evidence.

## Practice

Complete these on your own first.

1. In your own words, what does someone need to understand to succeed at “Context Clues for Vocabulary”?
2. Solve or explain one small item using today''s method. What is your result?
3. Name one mistake to avoid when practicing context clues for vocabulary.

## Check your work

Compare your answers only after you finish.

1. A clear paraphrase of the skill/definition — not only repeating the title.
2. Answers vary; must show correct use of the lesson method.
3. Examples: skipping definitions, unlabeled work, unsupported claims, or not checking.

## Stretch

Write 4–5 sentences or show a diagram that uses today''s skill, then revise one part for clarity.

Share your stretch work with a parent, teacher, or classmate if you can. Growth happens when we explain our thinking.

## Wrap-up

Review your notes, correct any practice misses, and mark the lesson complete when you can explain the main ideas without looking.','• Context Clues for Vocabulary means you can explain the idea in your own words and show it with an example.
• Strong language arts work shows steps: define → try a model → solve → check.
• A common mistake is rushing to an answer without using the definition. Slow down and label your work.',2,30,'section-1','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qgaSnqkwxLw');
