INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bwiy02jredan64zr609y','cmuh9bwix02jpedanwxcbfnzh','Multi-digit Multiplication','Build fluency with multi-digit multiplication using models, procedures, and unit labels.','Welcome, Grade 4 scholars.

This lesson focuses on **Multi-digit Multiplication**. Build fluency with multi-digit multiplication using models, procedures, and unit labels.

As you read, underline key words and try each practice item before checking the answers. Careful reading and clear thinking are the habits we build here.

## Learning goals

- Multi-digit Multiplication depends on place value and clear models (number lines, arrays, area models, or base-ten blocks as appropriate).
- Write each step: represent, compute, then interpret the answer with units.
- Estimate first to catch unreasonable results; check with an inverse operation when possible.

## Teach

### Key idea 1

Multi-digit Multiplication depends on place value and clear models (number lines, arrays, area models, or base-ten blocks as appropriate).

Write one sentence in your notebook that restates this idea in your own words.

### Key idea 2

Write each step: represent, compute, then interpret the answer with units.

Write one sentence in your notebook that restates this idea in your own words.

### Key idea 3

Estimate first to catch unreasonable results; check with an inverse operation when possible.

Write one sentence in your notebook that restates this idea in your own words.

## Guided practice notes

For Grade 4 Mathematics, strong habits matter as much as correct answers. Read each section twice. First, get the big idea. Second, hunt for details you can use in practice.

Texas classrooms often ask students to *show their work* and *explain their thinking*. Do the same here: write complete sentences when the question asks for explanation, and label diagrams clearly.

Common pitfall: rushing to the answer key. Cover the "Check your work" section until you have tried every item. Then correct in a different color so you can see growth.

## Worked example

Garden 8 ft by 5 ft: area = 8 × 5 = 40 square feet; perimeter = 2(8+5) = 26 feet. Always label square feet vs feet.

## Watch for this mistake

A frequent error in **Multi-digit Multiplication** is treating a slogan or a one-word label as mastery. Instead, restate the definition, show the worked example from memory, then complete practice with labeled steps or cited evidence.

## Practice

Complete these on your own first.

1. State one model you can draw for today''s skill.
2. Why label units on the final answer?
3. Name one check strategy after computing.

## Check your work

Compare your answers only after you finish.

1. Answers vary: array, number line, area model, tape diagram, etc., matched to the skill.
2. Units tell what the number measures and prevent mixing length with area/volume.
3. Estimate, inverse operation, or re-compute with a different method.

## Stretch

Write an original word problem requiring today''s skill. Solve on a separate page with a diagram.

Share your stretch work with a parent, teacher, or classmate if you can. Growth happens when we explain our thinking.

## Wrap-up

Review your notes, correct any practice misses, and mark the lesson complete when you can explain the main ideas without looking.','• Multi-digit Multiplication depends on place value and clear models (number lines, arrays, area models, or base-ten blocks as appropriate).
• Write each step: represent, compute, then interpret the answer with units.
• Estimate first to catch unreasonable results; check with an inverse operation when possible.',1,30,'section-1','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eg591BaGfOw');
