As you read, underline key words and try each practice item before checking the answers. Careful reading and clear thinking are the habits we build here.

## Learning goals

- Science explains patterns with models.
- Distinguish observation (what you see/measure) from inference (what you conclude).
- Link structure to function: why a part''s shape or arrangement enables what it does (cells, molecules, Earth systems, forces).

## Teach

### Key idea 1

Science explains patterns with models. For American Revolution Ideals, name the key parts/processes and how they interact.

Write one sentence in your notebook that restates this idea in your own words.

### Key idea 2

Distinguish observation (what you see/measure) from inference (what you conclude). Controlled comparisons improve investigations.

Write one sentence in your notebook that restates this idea in your own words.

### Key idea 3

Link structure to function: why a part''s shape or arrangement enables what it does (cells, molecules, Earth systems, forces).

Write one sentence in your notebook that restates this idea in your own words.

## Guided practice notes

For Grade 4 Social Studies, strong habits matter as much as correct answers. Read each section twice. First, get the big idea. Second, hunt for details you can use in practice.

Texas classrooms often ask students to *show their work* and *explain their thinking*. Do the same here: write complete sentences when the question asks for explanation, and label diagrams clearly.

Common pitfall: rushing to the answer key. Cover the "Check your work" section until you have tried every item. Then correct in a different color so you can see growth.

## Worked example

Worked model: identify inputs, processes, and outputs for American Revolution Ideals. Cite one observable piece of evidence and one inference a scientist might make.

## Watch for this mistake

A frequent error in **American Revolution Ideals** is treating a slogan or a one-word label as mastery. Instead, restate the definition, show the worked example from memory, then complete practice with labeled steps or cited evidence.

## Practice

Complete these on your own first.

1. Define one key term from today’s science focus in your own words.
2. Give one observation and one inference related to this topic.
3. Ask one testable question about american revolution ideals.

## Check your work

Compare your answers only after you finish.

1. Must match the lesson vocabulary (e.g., cell, evaporation, force) with a correct student paraphrase.
2. Observation = measurable/sensed; inference = conclusion based on evidence.
3. Question should be answerable with observation or experiment, not only opinion.

## Stretch

Draw and label a diagram for today''s topic. Add a caption explaining cause–effect in 3–4 sentences.

Share your stretch work with a parent, teacher, or classmate if you can. Growth happens when we explain our thinking.

## Wrap-up

Review your notes, correct any practice misses, and mark the lesson complete when you can explain the main ideas without looking.','• Science explains patterns with models. For American Revolution Ideals, name the key parts/processes and how they interact.
• Distinguish observation (what you see/measure) from inference (what you conclude). Controlled comparisons improve investigations.
• Link structure to function: why a part''s shape or arrangement enables what it does (cells, molecules, Earth systems, forces).',4,30,'section-2','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HlUiSBXQHCw');
INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bwm202w1edanxa8cw1dt','cmuh9bwlp02ufedanf1nr5vtw','U.S. Constitution Basics','Analyze u.s. constitution basics with maps, timelines, sourcing, and civic vocabulary.','Welcome, Grade 4 scholars.

This lesson focuses on **U.S. Constitution Basics**. Analyze u.s. constitution basics with maps, timelines, sourcing, and civic vocabulary.

As you read, underline key words and try each practice item before checking the answers. Careful reading and clear thinking are the habits we build here.

## Learning goals

- Ask who / what / when / where / why.
- Sources have authors, audiences, and purposes.
- Civic ideas (rights, responsibilities, constitutions, markets) connect past events to present participation.

## Teach

### Key idea 1

Ask who / what / when / where / why. Geography and resources shape settlement, conflict, and culture.

Write one sentence in your notebook that restates this idea in your own words.

### Key idea 2

Sources have authors, audiences, and purposes. Sourcing and corroboration reduce being misled by a single account.

Write one sentence in your notebook that restates this idea in your own words.

### Key idea 3

Civic ideas (rights, responsibilities, constitutions, markets) connect past events to present participation.

Write one sentence in your notebook that restates this idea in your own words.

## Guided practice notes

For Grade 4 Social Studies, strong habits matter as much as correct answers. Read each section twice. First, get the big idea. Second, hunt for details you can use in practice.

Texas classrooms often ask students to *show their work* and *explain their thinking*. Do the same here: write complete sentences when the question asks for explanation, and label diagrams clearly.

Common pitfall: rushing to the answer key. Cover the "Check your work" section until you have tried every item. Then correct in a different color so you can see growth.

## Worked example

The U.S. Constitution sets rules for government: separation of powers, federalism, and a process for amendments. When analyzing a clause, identify the power granted and any limits.

## Watch for this mistake

A frequent error in **U.S. Constitution Basics** is treating a slogan or a one-word label as mastery. Instead, restate the definition, show the worked example from memory, then complete practice with labeled steps or cited evidence.

## Practice

Complete these on your own first.

1. Name one fact and one opinion related to today’s topic.
2. Why does sourcing a document matter?
3. Connect this topic to life in Texas or the U.S. today in one sentence.

## Check your work

Compare your answers only after you finish.

1. Fact = verifiable; opinion = belief/value. Examples will vary but must be correctly typed.
2. Author, date, and purpose affect reliability and help you detect bias.
3. Any reasonable civic, geographic, economic, or cultural connection.

## Stretch

Create a mini timeline (4 events) or a sourced paragraph with a thesis and two pieces of evidence.

Share your stretch work with a parent, teacher, or classmate if you can. Growth happens when we explain our thinking.

## Wrap-up

Review your notes, correct any practice misses, and mark the lesson complete when you can explain the main ideas without looking.','• Ask who / what / when / where / why. Geography and resources shape settlement, conflict, and culture.
• Sources have authors, audiences, and purposes. Sourcing and corroboration reduce being misled by a single account.
• Civic ideas (rights, responsibilities, constitutions, markets) connect past events to present participation.',5,30,'section-2','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HsXlKBn9M9o');
