INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bwnc0310edanksgtq0ll','cmuh9bwmz02zsedanna85bwkc','Summarizing Informational Text','Find the main idea and supporting details; summarize without copying the whole text.','Welcome, Grade 5 scholars.

This lesson focuses on **Summarizing Informational Text**. Find the main idea and supporting details; summarize without copying the whole text.

As you read, underline key words and try each practice item before checking the answers. Careful reading and clear thinking are the habits we build here.

## Learning goals

- Main idea = the central point the paragraph or passage is mostly about.
- Summaries are shorter restatements in your own words — keep key points, drop examples that only decorate.
- A detail can be interesting yet not central.

## Teach

### Key idea 1

Main idea = the central point the paragraph or passage is mostly about. Details explain, prove, or illustrate that point.

Write one sentence in your notebook that restates this idea in your own words.

### Key idea 2

Summaries are shorter restatements in your own words — keep key points, drop examples that only decorate.

Write one sentence in your notebook that restates this idea in your own words.

### Key idea 3

A detail can be interesting yet not central. Ask: If I remove this sentence, does the main idea still stand?

Write one sentence in your notebook that restates this idea in your own words.

## Guided practice notes

For Grade 5 Language Arts, strong habits matter as much as correct answers. Read each section twice. First, get the big idea. Second, hunt for details you can use in practice.

Texas classrooms often ask students to *show their work* and *explain their thinking*. Do the same here: write complete sentences when the question asks for explanation, and label diagrams clearly.

Common pitfall: rushing to the answer key. Cover the "Check your work" section until you have tried every item. Then correct in a different color so you can see growth.

## Worked example

"Beavers build dams that slow water." Main idea: beavers change their habitat. Supporting detail: dams slow water. A summary might be: Beavers reshape streams by building dams that slow water flow.

## Watch for this mistake

A frequent error in **Summarizing Informational Text** is treating a slogan or a one-word label as mastery. Instead, restate the definition, show the worked example from memory, then complete practice with labeled steps or cited evidence.

## Practice

Complete these on your own first.

1. Is a title always the main idea?
2. What should a summary usually avoid?
3. Give one supporting detail for the main idea "Exercise helps mood."

## Check your work

Compare your answers only after you finish.

1. Not always — use the title as a clue, then confirm with the body sentences.
2. Long quotations, tiny trivia, and your personal opinions (unless asked).
3. Any relevant detail (e.g., endorphins, routine, sleep quality) — must support, not contradict.

## Stretch

Read a short informational paragraph. Write the main idea in one sentence and three supporting details as bullets, then a 2–3 sentence summary.

Share your stretch work with a parent, teacher, or classmate if you can. Growth happens when we explain our thinking.

## Wrap-up

Review your notes, correct any practice misses, and mark the lesson complete when you can explain the main ideas without looking.','• Main idea = the central point the paragraph or passage is mostly about. Details explain, prove, or illustrate that point.
• Summaries are shorter restatements in your own words — keep key points, drop examples that only decorate.
• A detail can be interesting yet not central. Ask: If I remove this sentence, does the main idea still stand?',4,30,'section-2','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AfXD9A9v2hM');
INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bwng031eedan99necyqv','cmuh9bwmz02zsedanna85bwkc','Figurative Language: Simile and Metaphor','Interpret figurative language and imagery; explain the effect on meaning and mood.','Welcome, Grade 5 scholars.

This lesson focuses on **Figurative Language: Simile and Metaphor**. Interpret figurative language and imagery; explain the effect on meaning and mood.

As you read, underline key words and try each practice item before checking the answers. Careful reading and clear thinking are the habits we build here.

## Learning goals

- Simile compares using like/as; metaphor says one thing *is* another.
- Ask: What two things are compared? What quality transfers? How does that shape mood or theme?.
- Sound devices (alliteration, repetition) and concrete sensory imagery work with figurative language to make ideas memorable.

## Teach

### Key idea 1

Simile compares using like/as; metaphor says one thing *is* another. Both create images that carry meaning beyond the literal.

Write one sentence in your notebook that restates this idea in your own words.

### Key idea 2

Ask: What two things are compared? What quality transfers? How does that shape mood or theme?

Write one sentence in your notebook that restates this idea in your own words.

### Key idea 3

Sound devices (alliteration, repetition) and concrete sensory imagery work with figurative language to make ideas memorable.

Write one sentence in your notebook that restates this idea in your own words.

## Guided practice notes

For Grade 5 Language Arts, strong habits matter as much as correct answers. Read each section twice. First, get the big idea. Second, hunt for details you can use in practice.

Texas classrooms often ask students to *show their work* and *explain their thinking*. Do the same here: write complete sentences when the question asks for explanation, and label diagrams clearly.

Common pitfall: rushing to the answer key. Cover the "Check your work" section until you have tried every item. Then correct in a different color so you can see growth.

## Worked example

"The classroom was a beehive" (metaphor) transfers busy, buzzing industry — not literal insects. Effect: the room feels energetic and noisy with purposeful motion.

## Watch for this mistake

A frequent error in **Figurative Language: Simile and Metaphor** is treating a slogan or a one-word label as mastery. Instead, restate the definition, show the worked example from memory, then complete practice with labeled steps or cited evidence.

## Practice

Complete these on your own first.

1. Label: "Her smile was like sunrise."
2. Why might an author prefer metaphor over a plain adjective?
3. Name one risk when interpreting figurative language.

## Check your work

Compare your answers only after you finish.

1. Simile (like) — compares smile to sunrise, suggesting warmth/hope.
2. To pack imagery and emotion into a compact comparison readers can picture.
3. Over-literal reading, or inventing meanings with no textual support.

## Stretch

Find two figurative phrases in a poem. For each: type, comparison, and effect on mood.

Share your stretch work with a parent, teacher, or classmate if you can. Growth happens when we explain our thinking.

## Wrap-up

Review your notes, correct any practice misses, and mark the lesson complete when you can explain the main ideas without looking.','• Simile compares using like/as; metaphor says one thing *is* another. Both create images that carry meaning beyond the literal.
• Ask: What two things are compared? What quality transfers? How does that shape mood or theme?
• Sound devices (alliteration, repetition) and concrete sensory imagery work with figurative language to make ideas memorable.',5,30,'section-2','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OrK9Onk6FfY');
