INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bx3u04saedan482hhrne','cmuh9bx3u04s8edana2dkfq5j','Proportional Relationships','Recognize proportional relationships; compute unit rates and solve with tables, equations, and graphs.','In this Grade 7 Mathematics lesson, we study **Proportional Relationships**.

Recognize proportional relationships; compute unit rates and solve with tables, equations, and graphs.

You will see key ideas, a worked example, practice questions, and a stretch challenge. Work carefully; these skills connect to the next lessons in your course.

## Learning goals

- Two quantities are proportional when their ratio stays constant.
- Unit rate is the amount of one quantity per 1 unit of another (miles per hour, price per item).
- Check proportion with equivalent ratios, a constant k in a table, or a line through the origin on a graph.

## Teach

### Concept 1

Two quantities are proportional when their ratio stays constant. The constant of proportionality k satisfies y = kx.

Pause and ask yourself: *When would I use this?* Note one connection to something you already know.

### Concept 2

Unit rate is the amount of one quantity per 1 unit of another (miles per hour, price per item).

Pause and ask yourself: *When would I use this?* Note one connection to something you already know.

### Concept 3

Check proportion with equivalent ratios, a constant k in a table, or a line through the origin on a graph.

Pause and ask yourself: *When would I use this?* Note one connection to something you already know.

## Why it matters

Mastering **Proportional Relationships** supports later coursework and everyday problem-solving. Students use these skills when they read news, manage money, evaluate claims, and participate as informed citizens.

Keep a short glossary of new terms from this lesson. Revisit it before your next live session.

## Study strategy

1. Preview headings.
2. Read actively (annotate).
3. Re-work the example without looking.
4. Complete practice, then check.
5. Attempt the stretch for depth.

If an item is wrong, write a one-sentence error analysis: what went wrong and how you will fix it next time.

## Worked example

Recipe: 3 cups flour for 12 muffins. Unit rate = 3/12 = 1/4 cup per muffin. For 20 muffins: 20 × 1/4 = 5 cups. Proportion check: 3/12 = x/20 → x = 5.

## Watch for this mistake

A frequent error in **Proportional Relationships** is treating a slogan or a one-word label as mastery. Instead, restate the definition, show the worked example from memory, then complete practice with labeled steps or cited evidence.

## Practice

Complete these on your own first.

1. Is the table (x,y): (2,6), (3,9), (5,15) proportional? What is k?
2. A car travels 150 miles in 3 hours at constant speed. Unit rate?
3. Does a line through (0,2) and (4,6) represent a proportional relationship?

## Check your work

Compare your answers only after you finish.

1. Yes — y/x = 3 each time, so k = 3 and y = 3x.
2. 50 miles per hour.
3. No — proportional graphs must pass through the origin (0,0).

## Stretch

Create two real-world tables — one proportional, one not. Explain how you know using ratios and a sketch graph.

Share your stretch work with a parent, teacher, or classmate if you can. Growth happens when we explain our thinking.

## Wrap-up

Review your notes, correct any practice misses, and mark the lesson complete when you can explain the main ideas without looking.','• Two quantities are proportional when their ratio stays constant. The constant of proportionality k satisfies y = kx.
• Unit rate is the amount of one quantity per 1 unit of another (miles per hour, price per item).
• Check proportion with equivalent ratios, a constant k in a table, or a line through the origin on a graph.',1,40,'section-1','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qYjiVWwefto');
INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bx3y04soedanhrdou3a1','cmuh9bx3u04s8edana2dkfq5j','Percent Increase and Decrease','Compute percent of a number, percent increase/decrease, and interpret percent in real contexts.','In this Grade 7 Mathematics lesson, we study **Percent Increase and Decrease**.

Compute percent of a number, percent increase/decrease, and interpret percent in real contexts.

You will see key ideas, a worked example, practice questions, and a stretch challenge. Work carefully; these skills connect to the next lessons in your course.

## Learning goals

- Percent means "per hundred.
- Percent increase: add the change to the original; percent decrease: subtract.
- Common errors: using the wrong base after multiple changes, or treating percent points the same as percent change.

## Teach

### Concept 1

Percent means "per hundred." To find p% of n, compute (p/100) × n.

Pause and ask yourself: *When would I use this?* Note one connection to something you already know.

### Concept 2

Percent increase: add the change to the original; percent decrease: subtract. Always ask: percent of *what* base?

Pause and ask yourself: *When would I use this?* Note one connection to something you already know.

### Concept 3

Common errors: using the wrong base after multiple changes, or treating percent points the same as percent change.

Pause and ask yourself: *When would I use this?* Note one connection to something you already know.

## Why it matters

Mastering **Percent Increase and Decrease** supports later coursework and everyday problem-solving. Students use these skills when they read news, manage money, evaluate claims, and participate as informed citizens.

Keep a short glossary of new terms from this lesson. Revisit it before your next live session.

## Study strategy

1. Preview headings.
2. Read actively (annotate).
3. Re-work the example without looking.
4. Complete practice, then check.
5. Attempt the stretch for depth.

If an item is wrong, write a one-sentence error analysis: what went wrong and how you will fix it next time.

## Worked example

A $80 jacket is discounted 25%. Discount = 0.25 × 80 = $20. Sale price = $60. If tax is then 8% of $60, tax = $4.80, total = $64.80.

## Watch for this mistake

A frequent error in **Percent Increase and Decrease** is treating a slogan or a one-word label as mastery. Instead, restate the definition, show the worked example from memory, then complete practice with labeled steps or cited evidence.

## Practice

Complete these on your own first.

1. What is 15% of 240?
2. A value rises from 50 to 65. Percent increase?
3. Why is "increase by 10%, then decrease by 10%" not back to the start?

## Check your work

Compare your answers only after you finish.

1. 36.
2. 15/50 = 0.30 = 30% increase.
3. The second 10% uses a new (higher) base, so you lose more than you gained.

## Stretch

Find a store ad with a percent off. Compute the sale price and explain the base you used.

Share your stretch work with a parent, teacher, or classmate if you can. Growth happens when we explain our thinking.

## Wrap-up

Review your notes, correct any practice misses, and mark the lesson complete when you can explain the main ideas without looking.','• Percent means "per hundred." To find p% of n, compute (p/100) × n.
• Percent increase: add the change to the original; percent decrease: subtract. Always ask: percent of *what* base?
• Common errors: using the wrong base after multiple changes, or treating percent points the same as percent change.',2,40,'section-1','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uej-8JLYb4k');
