INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bxh9067redang7prfxg7','cmuh9bxgg064ledanbgf7k6do','U.S. Civil Rights Milestones','Determine author''s purpose and tone; notice how word choice and structure persuade or inform.','In this Grade 8 Social Studies lesson, we study **U.S. Civil Rights Milestones**.

Determine author''s purpose and tone; notice how word choice and structure persuade or inform.

You will see key ideas, a worked example, practice questions, and a stretch challenge. Work carefully; these skills connect to the next lessons in your course.

## Learning goals

- Purpose is why the author wrote: to inform, persuade, entertain, or a blend.
- Diction, imagery, and organization are clues.
- Rhetorical appeals (ethos, pathos, logos) help explain *how* a speaker builds trust, emotion, or logic — always tie appeals back to specific lines.

## Teach

### Concept 1

Purpose is why the author wrote: to inform, persuade, entertain, or a blend. Tone is the author''s attitude toward the subject (urgent, sarcastic, respectful, alarmed).

Pause and ask yourself: *When would I use this?* Note one connection to something you already know.

### Concept 2

Diction, imagery, and organization are clues. Loaded words signal persuasion; neutral definitions and data often signal informing.

Pause and ask yourself: *When would I use this?* Note one connection to something you already know.

### Concept 3

Rhetorical appeals (ethos, pathos, logos) help explain *how* a speaker builds trust, emotion, or logic — always tie appeals back to specific lines.

Pause and ask yourself: *When would I use this?* Note one connection to something you already know.

## Why it matters

Mastering **U.S. Civil Rights Milestones** supports later coursework and everyday problem-solving. Students use these skills when they read news, manage money, evaluate claims, and participate as informed citizens.

Keep a short glossary of new terms from this lesson. Revisit it before your next live session.

## Study strategy

1. Preview headings.
2. Read actively (annotate).
3. Re-work the example without looking.
4. Complete practice, then check.
5. Attempt the stretch for depth.

If an item is wrong, write a one-sentence error analysis: what went wrong and how you will fix it next time.

## Worked example

A speech pairs graduation-rate statistics (logos) with a student''s story (pathos) and the speaker''s years in classrooms (ethos). Annotate each appeal, then judge whether the evidence is fair and sufficient for the claim.

## Watch for this mistake

A frequent error in **U.S. Civil Rights Milestones** is treating a slogan or a one-word label as mastery. Instead, restate the definition, show the worked example from memory, then complete practice with labeled steps or cited evidence.

## Practice

Complete these on your own first.

1. Purpose vs tone: which describes attitude?
2. Name one diction clue that a text may be trying to persuade.
3. Give one logos example and one pathos example you might find in an article.

## Check your work

Compare your answers only after you finish.

1. Tone describes attitude; purpose describes why the text was written.
2. Examples: should/must, loaded adjectives, us-vs-them framing, calls to action.
3. Logos: data/statistics; pathos: vivid personal story or emotionally charged imagery.

## Stretch

Annotate a one-page opinion piece for purpose, tone, and at least two rhetorical moves. Write a paragraph evaluating effectiveness.

Share your stretch work with a parent, teacher, or classmate if you can. Growth happens when we explain our thinking.

## Wrap-up

Review your notes, correct any practice misses, and mark the lesson complete when you can explain the main ideas without looking.','• Purpose is why the author wrote: to inform, persuade, entertain, or a blend. Tone is the author''s attitude toward the subject (urgent, sarcastic, respectful, alarmed).
• Diction, imagery, and organization are clues. Loaded words signal persuasion; neutral definitions and data often signal informing.
• Rhetorical appeals (ethos, pathos, logos) help explain *how* a speaker builds trust, emotion, or logic — always tie appeals back to specific lines.',9,40,'section-3','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=URxwe6LPvkM');
INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bxhv06a0edan7r9s8qwt','cmuh9bxhu069yedan7uay9q53','ACT Overview & Score Strategy','Learn the ACT structure, scoring, and a personal target-score plan.','Welcome to **ACT Prep** at Prosper Preparatory (Grade 8).

Today''s lesson: **ACT Overview & Score Strategy**. Learn the ACT structure, scoring, and a personal target-score plan.

Prosper Prep prepares students for **college athletic opportunities** and for **entrepreneurial / financial independence** after high school — whether or not they attend college.

## Learning goals
- The ACT has English, Math, Reading, Science, and an optional Writing section.
- Composite scores average the four multiple-choice sections; colleges publish middle-50% ranges.
- A paced study plan beats last-minute cramming — especially for student-athletes.

## Teach
### Concept 1

The ACT has English, Math, Reading, Science, and an optional Writing section.

Write a short note: how does this connect to athletics, college readiness, business, or character?
### Concept 2

Composite scores average the four multiple-choice sections; colleges publish middle-50% ranges.

Write a short note: how does this connect to athletics, college readiness, business, or character?
### Concept 3

A paced study plan beats last-minute cramming — especially for student-athletes.

Write a short note: how does this connect to athletics, college readiness, business, or character?


## Worked example

If your target composite is 24 and practice shows English 22, Math 20, Reading 25, Science 23, prioritize Math timing drills twice weekly while keeping Reading strong with daily passages.

## Practice

Complete these before peeking at answers.

1. Name the four required ACT multiple-choice sections.
2. Why set a target score?
3. True or false: Optional Writing always changes the composite.

## Check your work

1. English, Math, Reading, Science
2. It focuses practice on the sections that move the composite most.
3. False — Writing is scored separately.

## Stretch

Look up three colleges you care about and record their ACT middle-50% ranges. Set one realistic target and one stretch target.

## Wrap-up

Summarize today''s lesson in 4–6 sentences. Then complete the graded check below before the lesson can be marked complete.','• The ACT has English, Math, Reading, Science, and an optional Writing section.
• Composite scores average the four multiple-choice sections; colleges publish middle-50% ranges.
• A paced study plan beats last-minute cramming — especially for student-athletes.',1,45,'section-1','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JnNkFMRWbIM');
INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bxhy06aeedan4f6slunw','cmuh9bxhu069yedan7uay9q53','ACT English: Grammar & Rhetoric','Practice common grammar rules and rhetorical skills questions.','Welcome to **ACT Prep** at Prosper Preparatory (Grade 8).

Today''s lesson: **ACT English: Grammar & Rhetoric**. Practice common grammar rules and rhetorical skills questions.

Prosper Prep prepares students for **college athletic opportunities** and for **entrepreneurial / financial independence** after high school — whether or not they attend college.

## Learning goals
- ACT English rewards concise, clear writing and standard conventions.
- Watch subject-verb agreement, pronouns, commas, and redundancy.
- Rhetoric items ask which choice best achieves a stated purpose.

## Teach

### Teaching point 1

Purpose is why the author wrote: to inform, persuade, entertain, or a blend. Tone is the author''s attitude toward the subject (urgent, sarcastic, respectful, alarmed).

### Teaching point 2

Diction, imagery, and organization are clues. Loaded words signal persuasion; neutral definitions and data often signal informing.

### Teaching point 3

Rhetorical appeals (ethos, pathos, logos) help explain *how* a speaker builds trust, emotion, or logic — always tie appeals back to specific lines.

### Concept 1

ACT English rewards concise, clear writing and standard conventions.

Write a short note: how does this connect to athletics, college readiness, business, or character?
### Concept 2

Watch subject-verb agreement, pronouns, commas, and redundancy.

Write a short note: how does this connect to athletics, college readiness, business, or character?
### Concept 3

Rhetoric items ask which choice best achieves a stated purpose.

Write a short note: how does this connect to athletics, college readiness, business, or character?


## Worked example

Sentence: "The players, who trains daily, improves." Error: who/trains should match plural players → "who train" and verb "improve." On rhetoric, if the goal is "add a specific detail," pick the choice with concrete information, not a vague claim.

## Practice

Complete these before peeking at answers.

1. Fix: "Each of the athletes have a plan."
2. What do rhetoric questions prioritize?
3. Name two punctuation issues ACT loves.

## Check your work

1. Each … has a plan (each is singular).
2. Purpose, audience, clarity, and organization — not just "sounds fancy."
3. Commas with nonessential clauses; apostrophes; run-ons / comma splices.

## Stretch

Complete a 15-question English timed set (or a released practice chunk). Log every miss by rule type.

## Wrap-up

Summarize today''s lesson in 4–6 sentences. Then complete the graded check below before the lesson can be marked complete.','• ACT English rewards concise, clear writing and standard conventions.
• Watch subject-verb agreement, pronouns, commas, and redundancy.
• Rhetoric items ask which choice best achieves a stated purpose.',2,45,'section-1','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ytVgcQZ9w5A');
INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bxi106asedan3tb8y1m3','cmuh9bxhu069yedan7uay9q53','ACT Math: Shortcuts & Timing','Build speed with algebra, geometry, and calculator discipline.','Welcome to **ACT Prep** at Prosper Preparatory (Grade 8).

Today''s lesson: **ACT Math: Shortcuts & Timing**. Build speed with algebra, geometry, and calculator discipline.

Prosper Prep prepares students for **college athletic opportunities** and for **entrepreneurial / financial independence** after high school — whether or not they attend college.

## Learning goals
- ACT Math increases in difficulty; early items should be quick points.
- Translate word problems into equations before computing.
- Know when to estimate, plug in answers, or draw a figure.

## Teach

### Teaching point 1

Know the section structure and scoring. Target scores focus practice on the highest-leverage weaknesses.

### Teaching point 2

For reading/English: answer from the passage/rules, not outside stories. For math: translate → represent → solve → check.

### Teaching point 3

Error logs beat random volume: tag each miss by skill and redo a twin item the next day.

### Concept 1

ACT Math increases in difficulty; early items should be quick points.

Write a short note: how does this connect to athletics, college readiness, business, or character?
### Concept 2

Translate word problems into equations before computing.

Write a short note: how does this connect to athletics, college readiness, business, or character?
### Concept 3

Know when to estimate, plug in answers, or draw a figure.

Write a short note: how does this connect to athletics, college readiness, business, or character?


## Worked example

A ratio problem: 3:5 parts mix to 40 ounces total. Parts = 8, so one part = 5 oz → 15 and 25. Checking answer choices by plugging often saves algebra time.

## Practice

Complete these before peeking at answers.

1. Why skip and return on hard mid-test items?
2. Give one ACT Math strategy besides "solve fully."
3. What should you bring besides a pencil?

## Check your work

1. Protect easy points and avoid time traps.
2. Plug in answer choices, pick numbers, estimate, or draw.
3. An approved calculator you know well; spare batteries if needed.

## Stretch

Time yourself on 15 mixed algebra/geometry items. Aim for accuracy first, then shave seconds.

## Wrap-up

Summarize today''s lesson in 4–6 sentences. Then complete the graded check below before the lesson can be marked complete.','• ACT Math increases in difficulty; early items should be quick points.
• Translate word problems into equations before computing.
• Know when to estimate, plug in answers, or draw a figure.',3,45,'section-1','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9DxrF6Tt0Aw');
