INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bxmu06uyedans5me33re','cmuh9bxmu06uwedanteslfmb4','Close Reading of Complex Prose','Learn Close Reading of Complex Prose through definitions, a worked example, guided practice, and a quick error check for Grade 9 Language Arts.','This Grade 9 Language Arts lesson develops **Close Reading of Complex Prose** with college-ready rigor.

Learn Close Reading of Complex Prose through definitions, a worked example, guided practice, and a quick error check for Grade 9 Language Arts.

Read actively: annotate definitions, follow the worked example step by step, then complete practice before the stretch problem.

## Learning goals

- Close Reading of Complex Prose requires precise definitions, justified procedures, and attention to edge cases or counterexamples.
- College-ready work cites evidence or shows algebraic/scientific reasoning — not slogans.
- Transfer the skill: invent a second original example and explain why the same method works.

## Teach

### Core concept 1

Close Reading of Complex Prose requires precise definitions, justified procedures, and attention to edge cases or counterexamples.

Consider edge cases and counterexamples. Strong reasoning means knowing both when a rule applies and when it does not.

### Core concept 2

College-ready work cites evidence or shows algebraic/scientific reasoning — not slogans.

Consider edge cases and counterexamples. Strong reasoning means knowing both when a rule applies and when it does not.

### Core concept 3

Transfer the skill: invent a second original example and explain why the same method works.

Consider edge cases and counterexamples. Strong reasoning means knowing both when a rule applies and when it does not.

## Why it matters

**Close Reading of Complex Prose** sits inside a larger academic sequence. Treat this lesson as deliberate practice toward graduation-ready literacy, quantitative reasoning, scientific literacy, or civic competence.

Students compete nationally when they can write clearly, model quantitatively, evaluate evidence, and participate knowledgeably in civic life. Online learning at Prosper Preparatory expects the same seriousness as a strong brick-and-mortar classroom.

## Academic habits

- Paraphrase definitions before memorizing them.
- Connect today''s example to a second original example you invent.
- Track misconceptions in a running "error log."
- Bring one precise question to your next live session or office hours.

## Transfer task

Write a short reflection (6–10 sentences): How does **Close Reading of Complex Prose** appear outside school — in work, media, family decisions, or local issues? Cite at least one concrete scenario.

## Worked example

Worked example for Close Reading of Complex Prose: (1) Restate the problem. (2) Identify the rule or definition that applies. (3) Carry out the steps with labels. (4) Check with an estimate, inverse operation, or evidence citation.

## Watch for this mistake

A frequent error in **Close Reading of Complex Prose** is treating a slogan or a one-word label as mastery. Instead, restate the definition, show the worked example from memory, then complete practice with labeled steps or cited evidence.

## Practice

Complete these on your own first.

1. In your own words, what does someone need to understand to succeed at “Close Reading of Complex Prose”?
2. Solve or explain one small item using today''s method. What is your result?
3. Name one mistake to avoid when practicing close reading of complex prose.

## Check your work

Compare your answers only after you finish.

1. A clear paraphrase of the skill/definition — not only repeating the title.
2. Answers vary; must show correct use of the lesson method.
3. Examples: skipping definitions, unlabeled work, unsupported claims, or not checking.

## Stretch

Create an original problem or short analysis using close reading of complex prose. Solve it and explain your reasoning in 6–8 sentences.

Share your stretch work with a parent, teacher, or classmate if you can. Growth happens when we explain our thinking.

## Wrap-up

Review your notes, correct any practice misses, and mark the lesson complete when you can explain the main ideas without looking.','• Close Reading of Complex Prose requires precise definitions, justified procedures, and attention to edge cases or counterexamples.
• College-ready work cites evidence or shows algebraic/scientific reasoning — not slogans.
• Transfer the skill: invent a second original example and explain why the same method works.',1,45,'section-1','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3PDnti9X8LR');
INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bxmx06vcedandjgv33tp','cmuh9bxmu06uwedanteslfmb4','Rhetorical Appeals in Speeches','Determine author''s purpose and tone; notice how word choice and structure persuade or inform.','This Grade 9 Language Arts lesson develops **Rhetorical Appeals in Speeches** with college-ready rigor.

Determine author''s purpose and tone; notice how word choice and structure persuade or inform.

Read actively: annotate definitions, follow the worked example step by step, then complete practice before the stretch problem.

## Learning goals

- Purpose is why the author wrote: to inform, persuade, entertain, or a blend.
- Diction, imagery, and organization are clues.
- Rhetorical appeals (ethos, pathos, logos) help explain *how* a speaker builds trust, emotion, or logic — always tie appeals back to specific lines.

## Teach

### Core concept 1

Purpose is why the author wrote: to inform, persuade, entertain, or a blend. Tone is the author''s attitude toward the subject (urgent, sarcastic, respectful, alarmed).

Consider edge cases and counterexamples. Strong reasoning means knowing both when a rule applies and when it does not.

### Core concept 2

Diction, imagery, and organization are clues. Loaded words signal persuasion; neutral definitions and data often signal informing.

Consider edge cases and counterexamples. Strong reasoning means knowing both when a rule applies and when it does not.

### Core concept 3

Rhetorical appeals (ethos, pathos, logos) help explain *how* a speaker builds trust, emotion, or logic — always tie appeals back to specific lines.

Consider edge cases and counterexamples. Strong reasoning means knowing both when a rule applies and when it does not.

## Why it matters

**Rhetorical Appeals in Speeches** sits inside a larger academic sequence. Treat this lesson as deliberate practice toward graduation-ready literacy, quantitative reasoning, scientific literacy, or civic competence.

Students compete nationally when they can write clearly, model quantitatively, evaluate evidence, and participate knowledgeably in civic life. Online learning at Prosper Preparatory expects the same seriousness as a strong brick-and-mortar classroom.

## Academic habits

- Paraphrase definitions before memorizing them.
- Connect today''s example to a second original example you invent.
- Track misconceptions in a running "error log."
- Bring one precise question to your next live session or office hours.

## Transfer task

Write a short reflection (6–10 sentences): How does **Rhetorical Appeals in Speeches** appear outside school — in work, media, family decisions, or local issues? Cite at least one concrete scenario.

## Worked example

A speech pairs graduation-rate statistics (logos) with a student''s story (pathos) and the speaker''s years in classrooms (ethos). Annotate each appeal, then judge whether the evidence is fair and sufficient for the claim.

## Watch for this mistake

A frequent error in **Rhetorical Appeals in Speeches** is treating a slogan or a one-word label as mastery. Instead, restate the definition, show the worked example from memory, then complete practice with labeled steps or cited evidence.

## Practice

Complete these on your own first.

1. Purpose vs tone: which describes attitude?
2. Name one diction clue that a text may be trying to persuade.
3. Give one logos example and one pathos example you might find in an article.

## Check your work

Compare your answers only after you finish.

1. Tone describes attitude; purpose describes why the text was written.
2. Examples: should/must, loaded adjectives, us-vs-them framing, calls to action.
3. Logos: data/statistics; pathos: vivid personal story or emotionally charged imagery.

## Stretch

Annotate a one-page opinion piece for purpose, tone, and at least two rhetorical moves. Write a paragraph evaluating effectiveness.

Share your stretch work with a parent, teacher, or classmate if you can. Growth happens when we explain our thinking.

## Wrap-up

Review your notes, correct any practice misses, and mark the lesson complete when you can explain the main ideas without looking.','• Purpose is why the author wrote: to inform, persuade, entertain, or a blend. Tone is the author''s attitude toward the subject (urgent, sarcastic, respectful, alarmed).
• Diction, imagery, and organization are clues. Loaded words signal persuasion; neutral definitions and data often signal informing.
• Rhetorical appeals (ethos, pathos, logos) help explain *how* a speaker builds trust, emotion, or logic — always tie appeals back to specific lines.',2,45,'section-1','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o85OkeKw1j0');
