INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9by2608lgedanxceuikp2','cmuh9by2508leedan8cb09q8z','Linear Functions Deep Dive','Model linear relationships with slope-intercept, point-slope, and standard forms.','This Grade 10 Mathematics lesson develops **Linear Functions Deep Dive** with college-ready rigor.

Model linear relationships with slope-intercept, point-slope, and standard forms.

## Learning goals
- Slope m = Δy/Δx measures constant rate of change.
- y = mx + b makes intercept and rate visible for graphing and modeling.
- Equivalent forms represent the same line; choose the form that fits the given data.

## Teach
### Core concept 1

Slope m = Δy/Δx measures constant rate of change.

Consider edge cases and counterexamples. Strong reasoning means knowing both when a rule applies and when it does not.
### Core concept 2

y = mx + b makes intercept and rate visible for graphing and modeling.

Consider edge cases and counterexamples. Strong reasoning means knowing both when a rule applies and when it does not.
### Core concept 3

Equivalent forms represent the same line; choose the form that fits the given data.

Consider edge cases and counterexamples. Strong reasoning means knowing both when a rule applies and when it does not.

## Why it matters

**Linear Functions Deep Dive** sits inside a larger Grade 10 academic sequence at Prosper Preparatory. Treat this lesson as deliberate practice toward graduation-ready literacy or quantitative reasoning.

## Worked example

Through (2, 5) with slope −3: y − 5 = −3(x − 2) → y = −3x + 11. Check: at x = 2, y = 5.

## Practice

Complete these on your own first.

1. Write point-slope for slope 2 through (−1, 4).
2. Convert y − 4 = 2(x + 1) to slope-intercept.
3. What does b represent in y = mx + b?

## Check your work

1. y − 4 = 2(x + 1)
2. y = 2x + 6
3. The y-intercept (value when x = 0)

## Stretch

Fit a linear model to three real data points from a news chart and discuss residuals qualitatively.

## Wrap-up

Review your notes, correct any practice misses, and mark the lesson complete when you can explain the main ideas without looking.','• Slope m = Δy/Δx measures constant rate of change.
• y = mx + b makes intercept and rate visible for graphing and modeling.
• Equivalent forms represent the same line; choose the form that fits the given data.',1,45,'section-1','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VhokQhjl5t0');
INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9by2908luedano8n4m0fz','cmuh9by2508leedan8cb09q8z','Systems of Linear Equations','Solve 2×2 systems by graphing, substitution, and elimination; classify solution types.','This Grade 10 Mathematics lesson develops **Systems of Linear Equations** with college-ready rigor.

Solve 2×2 systems by graphing, substitution, and elimination; classify solution types.

## Learning goals
- A solution is an ordered pair that satisfies both equations.
- Elimination and substitution are algebraic; graphing shows intersection.
- Parallel distinct lines → no solution; coincident lines → infinitely many.

## Teach
### Core concept 1

A solution is an ordered pair that satisfies both equations.

Consider edge cases and counterexamples. Strong reasoning means knowing both when a rule applies and when it does not.
### Core concept 2

Elimination and substitution are algebraic; graphing shows intersection.

Consider edge cases and counterexamples. Strong reasoning means knowing both when a rule applies and when it does not.
### Core concept 3

Parallel distinct lines → no solution; coincident lines → infinitely many.

Consider edge cases and counterexamples. Strong reasoning means knowing both when a rule applies and when it does not.

## Why it matters

**Systems of Linear Equations** sits inside a larger Grade 10 academic sequence at Prosper Preparatory. Treat this lesson as deliberate practice toward graduation-ready literacy or quantitative reasoning.

## Worked example

x + y = 10 and x − y = 2 → add: 2x = 12 → x = 6, y = 4.

## Practice

Complete these on your own first.

1. Solve: y = 2x, x + y = 9.
2. Two parallel lines with different intercepts have how many solutions?
3. Name one algebraic method besides elimination.

## Check your work

1. x = 3, y = 6
2. None
3. Substitution

## Stretch

Write a word problem whose system has no solution; explain why graphically.

## Wrap-up

Review your notes, correct any practice misses, and mark the lesson complete when you can explain the main ideas without looking.','• A solution is an ordered pair that satisfies both equations.
• Elimination and substitution are algebraic; graphing shows intersection.
• Parallel distinct lines → no solution; coincident lines → infinitely many.',2,45,'section-1','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nok99JOhcjo');
