INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bya409hqedank6v6zw4w','cmuh9by9o09fqedanaqm4dg0q','Advanced Math & Geometry Essentials','Quadratics, exponents, and geometry relationships that appear often.','Welcome to **SAT Prep** at Prosper Preparatory (Grade 10).

Today''s lesson: **Advanced Math & Geometry Essentials**. Quadratics, exponents, and geometry relationships that appear often.

Prosper Prep prepares students for **college athletic opportunities** and for **entrepreneurial / financial independence** after high school — whether or not they attend college.

## Learning goals
- Factoring and quadratic formula are complementary tools.
- Exponent rules simplify before expanding everything.
- Right-triangle and circle relationships appear in applied settings.

## Teach

### Teaching point 1

Know the section structure and scoring. Target scores focus practice on the highest-leverage weaknesses.

### Teaching point 2

For reading/English: answer from the passage/rules, not outside stories. For math: translate → represent → solve → check.

### Teaching point 3

Error logs beat random volume: tag each miss by skill and redo a twin item the next day.

### Concept 1

Factoring and quadratic formula are complementary tools.

Write a short note: how does this connect to athletics, college readiness, business, or character?
### Concept 2

Exponent rules simplify before expanding everything.

Write a short note: how does this connect to athletics, college readiness, business, or character?
### Concept 3

Right-triangle and circle relationships appear in applied settings.

Write a short note: how does this connect to athletics, college readiness, business, or character?


## Worked example

x² − 5x + 6 = 0 factors to (x−2)(x−3)=0 → x=2 or 3. For 3-4-5 triangles, scale carefully.

## Practice

Complete these before peeking at answers.

1. Solutions to (x−4)(x+1)=0?
2. Simplify x³ · x².
3. In a right triangle, what does Pythagorean theorem state?

## Check your work

1. x=4 or x=−1
2. x⁵
3. a² + b² = c² for legs a,b and hypotenuse c

## Stretch

Solve two quadratic practice items two ways (factor and formula) and compare.

## Wrap-up

Summarize today''s lesson in 4–6 sentences. Then complete the graded check below before the lesson can be marked complete.','• Factoring and quadratic formula are complementary tools.
• Exponent rules simplify before expanding everything.
• Right-triangle and circle relationships appear in applied settings.',6,45,'section-2','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JnNkFMRWbIM');
INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bya709i4edanupjeus60','cmuh9by9o09fqedanaqm4dg0q','Calculator & No-Calculator Discipline','Know when technology helps — and when it slows you down.','Welcome to **SAT Prep** at Prosper Preparatory (Grade 10).

Today''s lesson: **Calculator & No-Calculator Discipline**. Know when technology helps — and when it slows you down.

Prosper Prep prepares students for **college athletic opportunities** and for **entrepreneurial / financial independence** after high school — whether or not they attend college.

## Learning goals
- Mental math and structure beat button-mashing.
- Estimate to catch calculator typos.
- Practice both modes so neither surprises you.

## Teach

### Teaching point 1

Know the section structure and scoring. Target scores focus practice on the highest-leverage weaknesses.

### Teaching point 2

For reading/English: answer from the passage/rules, not outside stories. For math: translate → represent → solve → check.

### Teaching point 3

Error logs beat random volume: tag each miss by skill and redo a twin item the next day.

### Concept 1

Mental math and structure beat button-mashing.

Write a short note: how does this connect to athletics, college readiness, business, or character?
### Concept 2

Estimate to catch calculator typos.

Write a short note: how does this connect to athletics, college readiness, business, or character?
### Concept 3

Practice both modes so neither surprises you.

Write a short note: how does this connect to athletics, college readiness, business, or character?


## Worked example

Before calculating 19.8% of 205, estimate 20% of 200 = 40. If the calculator shows 4.059, you likely missed a decimal.

## Practice

Complete these before peeking at answers.

1. Name one risk of overusing a calculator.
2. Why estimate first?
3. What should you practice without a calculator?

## Check your work

1. Slower pace; decimal errors; losing algebraic insight
2. Sanity-check the result
3. Fractions, simple linear equations, arithmetic fluency

## Stretch

Do a 10-item mixed set: five with calculator allowed, five without; reflect on timing.

## Wrap-up

Summarize today''s lesson in 4–6 sentences. Then complete the graded check below before the lesson can be marked complete.','• Mental math and structure beat button-mashing.
• Estimate to catch calculator typos.
• Practice both modes so neither surprises you.',7,45,'section-3','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JnNkFMRWbIM');
INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9byaa09iiedanap33gaer','cmuh9by9o09fqedanaqm4dg0q','Full-Length Practice Review','Convert a practice test into a two-week improvement plan.','Welcome to **SAT Prep** at Prosper Preparatory (Grade 10).

