Before you leave this video, rehearse the decision out loud once: name the skill, name the trap, name the fix. That three-beat habit is what survives on test day when adrenaline flattens careful reading. If you miss an item in practice, do not just glance at the key—write the trap name in your error log and schedule a five-item mini-set on that trap within forty-eight hours. Spaced retrieval beats rereading the same explanation. Athletes already periodize training; do the same for English defects. One more practical tip: when two choices both seem grammatical, ask which one better preserves the paragraph''s purpose and sheds empty words. That tie-breaker resolves a surprising number of late-section hesitations without burning another thirty seconds of rereading. Keep your pencil moving, keep your reasons named, and keep your composite levers honest.

## Wrap-up
Complete the graded check.
','• Eliminate by named wrong reasons
• Use two-pass elimination under time
• Catalog personal frequent traps',17,35,'module-e4',NULL);
INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bz070ccjedanfjzh9hd0','cmuh9byyz0c7redandrerxelc','E4.3 Timed English section drill','Full-section pacing callouts, error logging, and written fix-and-justify.','# E4.3 Timed English section drill

## Objectives
- Complete a timed English section drill with checkpoints
- Log misses by content/trap/timing
- Rewrite a sentence and justify the rule in writing

## Teach
### How to run the drill
1. Use **ACT English Section Drill 1** (or 2) on the course page under timed conditions (45 minutes if full 75).
2. Callouts: check clock at items ~15, 30, 45, 60.
3. Bubble as you go; no mid-section phone breaks.
4. Afterward, tag every miss: **content / trap / timing**.

### Live pacing callouts
- Behind at item 30? Shorten deliberation now.
- Last 3 minutes: ensure no blanks; return to marked rhetoric.

### Written fix-and-justify
Rewrite one flawed sentence correctly and explain the governing rules for teacher scoring.

### Worked mindset
A drill without an error log is entertainment. A drill with tags is training.

<!-- WRITTEN_PROMPT:English fix-and-justify|10 -->
Fix-and-justify (teacher-scored, max 10 points).

Sentence to repair:
"Waiting for the starter''s pistol, the track was silent, the sprinters whom was in lane three almost false started due to the fact that they was nervous."

Your task (about 150–250 words):
1. Rewrite the sentence (or split into two) so it is clear, grammatical, and concise.
2. Name at least three distinct rules/issues you fixed (e.g., dangling modifier, comma splice, who/whom, agreement, concision).
3. For each issue, explain in one or two sentences why your revision is correct on ACT English terms.
4. End with one pacing note: how you would triage a messy sentence like this under a 36-second average.

Write in paragraphs. Specific beats vague.
<!-- /WRITTEN_PROMPT -->


## Practice
1. Complete Drill 1 timed; tag misses the same day.
2. Re-do only tagged timing misses untimed—confirm they were pacing, not content.
3. Submit the written fix-and-justify.



## Video script (Prosper Prep original)

*[Spoken narration ~750–900 words — instructional video authoring script; not a claim that video is filmed.]*

"Welcome to Prosper Prep ACT English. Today''s focus is Timed English wrap-up. I am going to teach the rule, show a worked ACT-style item, give you a pause to try one, then replay the trap so you do not donate easy points under a thirty-six-second average.

This lesson wraps Module E with a timed English section drill and a written fix-and-justify. Set up like test day. Forty-five minutes for a full seventy-five-item drill. Hit callouts near items fifteen, thirty, forty-five, and sixty. When the timer ends, stop. Tag every miss as content, trap, or timing. The written assignment stacks defects—dangling modifier, splice, who/whom, agreement, wordiness—and asks you to justify each fix with a named rule. Teachers score clarity of rewrite and accuracy of justification. After Drill 1, schedule Drill 2 later so improvement is measured.

Under time pressure your ear will lie. Your pencil should not. Cross out interrupters, name the relationship before you pick a transition, and vertical-scan choice endings before you reread a whole paragraph. Elite English is not fancier vocabulary; it is faster, cleaner decisions with named reasons.

Pause the video. Say the rule in one sentence. Then complete the graded check below. Keep an error-log tag ready—content, trap, or timing—so this lesson becomes a lever, not a mood. Remember: Prosper Prep items are original; we do not rehost ACT Incorporated forms. Official practice is link-out or school-licensed only. Train integrity the same way you train grammar.

 One last rehearsal: cover the answer choices, predict the edit, uncover, eliminate by named defect, and only then commit. If you cannot name the defect, you are guessing earlier than necessary. Build that naming reflex until it feels automatic—even boring. Boring under practice is calm under the clock. Finally, connect this skill to Module E pacing. A perfect rule that costs you two minutes on one item can still drop your section score if passage five becomes a blank field. Use now/later/guess. Bank clean usage points. Return to sticky rhetoric with a smaller clock and a sharper elimination tree. That combination—content plus strategy—is how composites climb toward the thirty-three-plus stretch band without pretending every item deserves a thesis."




### Pacing callout script (say aloud during Drill 1)
- Item 15: "Am I near nine minutes used?"
- Item 30: "Halfway map—if behind, shorten rhetoric now."
- Item 45: "Protect the last two passages."
- Item 60: "No blanks left behind; guess with elimination."
- Final 3 minutes: bubble remaining, then return to marked items only.

### After-action review template
| Item # | Skill tag | Miss type (content/trap/timing) | Next drill |
|---|---|---|---|
|  |  |  |  |

Fill at least eight rows the same day. Same-day tags beat weekend mythology.

### Why written fix-and-justify belongs here
Conscious competence is the bridge between knowing a rule in a lesson and applying it at second thirty-four of a messy sentence. Writing the repair and naming the rules forces retrieval under mild pressure—closer to test day than silent nodding at an answer key.



Before you leave this video, rehearse the decision out loud once: name the skill, name the trap, name the fix. That three-beat habit is what survives on test day when adrenaline flattens careful reading. If you miss an item in practice, do not just glance at the key—write the trap name in your error log and schedule a five-item mini-set on that trap within forty-eight hours. Spaced retrieval beats rereading the same explanation. Athletes already periodize training; do the same for English defects. One more practical tip: when two choices both seem grammatical, ask which one better preserves the paragraph''s purpose and sheds empty words. That tie-breaker resolves a surprising number of late-section hesitations without burning another thirty seconds of rereading. Keep your pencil moving, keep your reasons named, and keep your composite levers honest.

## Wrap-up
Complete the graded check , run a timed English drill, and submit the fix-and-justify.
','• Complete a timed English section drill with checkpoints
• Log misses by content/trap/timing
• Rewrite a sentence and justify the rule in writing',18,50,'module-e4',NULL);
