Prosper Prep prepares students for **college athletic opportunities** and for **entrepreneurial / financial independence** after high school — whether or not they attend college.

## Learning goals
- College athletic pathways include NCAA divisions, NAIA, and other associations — rules differ.
- Core-course planning, GPA, and amateurism concepts matter years before signing anything.
- This course is educational awareness only — always verify with official sources and school counselors.

## Teach
### Concept 1

College athletic pathways include NCAA divisions, NAIA, and other associations — rules differ.

Write a short note: how does this connect to athletics, college readiness, business, or character?
### Concept 2

Core-course planning, GPA, and amateurism concepts matter years before signing anything.

Write a short note: how does this connect to athletics, college readiness, business, or character?
### Concept 3

This course is educational awareness only — always verify with official sources and school counselors.

Write a short note: how does this connect to athletics, college readiness, business, or character?


## Worked example

Example: a student maps graduation requirements + likely core courses by sophomore year, then checks an official eligibility center checklist with a counselor — never relying on rumors from social media.

## Practice

Complete these before peeking at answers.

1. Is this class official NCAA legal advice?
2. Name one association athletes may hear about.
3. Who should you ask about your personal plan?

## Check your work

1. No — educational overview only; verify with official sources/counselors
2. NCAA, NAIA, NJCAA, or similar — rules vary
3. Counselor, coach, family — and official published requirements

## Stretch

With a counselor or coach, list three official resources (sites/handbooks) you will use for eligibility questions.

## Wrap-up

Summarize today''s lesson in 4–6 sentences. Then complete the graded check below before the lesson can be marked complete.','• College athletic pathways include NCAA divisions, NAIA, and other associations — rules differ.
• Core-course planning, GPA, and amateurism concepts matter years before signing anything.
• This course is educational awareness only — always verify with official sources and school counselors.',1,45,'section-1','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oqMVx6xMqjE');
INSERT INTO "Lesson" VALUES('cmuh9bz6k0d3dedanazcmyi1g','cmuh9bz6h0d2xedanaoeawuya','Time Management for Training + School','Build calendars that protect practice, film, homework, and rest.','Welcome to **College Athletic Pathway** at Prosper Preparatory (Grade 12).

Today''s lesson: **Time Management for Training + School**. Build calendars that protect practice, film, homework, and rest.

Prosper Prep prepares students for **college athletic opportunities** and for **entrepreneurial / financial independence** after high school — whether or not they attend college.

## Learning goals
- Put fixed commitments first (school, practice, travel).
- Use short deep-work blocks for hard academics.
- Recovery (sleep, nutrition, mental rest) is part of performance.

## Teach

### Teaching point 1

Athletic progress pairs deliberate practice with recovery, nutrition basics, and injury prevention.

### Teaching point 2

College opportunity depends on film/skills *and* academics, character, and eligibility rules.

### Teaching point 3

Track measurable goals (times, lifts, skills) weekly; adjust load when form breaks down.

### Concept 1

Put fixed commitments first (school, practice, travel).

Write a short note: how does this connect to athletics, college readiness, business, or character?
### Concept 2

Use short deep-work blocks for hard academics.

Write a short note: how does this connect to athletics, college readiness, business, or character?
### Concept 3

Recovery (sleep, nutrition, mental rest) is part of performance.

Write a short note: how does this connect to athletics, college readiness, business, or character?


## Worked example

Sunday 20-minute planning: color-code practice, lifts, SAT/ACT blocks, and project due dates. If a tournament weekend hits, shift academic deep work earlier in the week.

## Practice

Complete these before peeking at answers.

1. What goes on the calendar first?
2. Why protect sleep?
3. What is a deep-work block?

## Check your work

1. Fixed obligations
2. Learning, injury resilience, mood, and performance
3. Distraction-free time for hard cognitive tasks

## Stretch

Build a one-week calendar including training and two academic priorities.

## Wrap-up

Summarize today''s lesson in 4–6 sentences. Then complete the graded check below before the lesson can be marked complete.','• Put fixed commitments first (school, practice, travel).
• Use short deep-work blocks for hard academics.
