INSERT INTO "Question" VALUES('cmuh9bwsd03lcedantffsump8','cmuh9bwsd03laedanptmprhap',NULL,'MULTIPLE_CHOICE','In literary analysis, which option correctly distinguishes theme from topic?','["Topic and theme are identical — both are one-word labels for the plot.","Theme is only the author''s biography; topic is the setting.","Topic is a short subject label (e.g., belonging); theme is a full claim the story develops about that subject.","Theme is always the first sentence of the story; topic is the last sentence."]',2,'Topic names the subject area; theme states what the story suggests about it.',1,1);
INSERT INTO "Question" VALUES('cmuh9bwse03leedanamd87u3d','cmuh9bwsd03laedanptmprhap',NULL,'MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Based on "The Relay," which statement is the strongest theme (not a topic or plot summary)?','["Jordan ran track in seventh grade.","Sports.","Maya is faster than Jordan, so starters never matter.","Choosing the team''s success over personal spotlight can create a more lasting pride."]',3,'The strong option is a generalizable claim supported by Jordan''s choice and response; the others are plot, topic, or unsupported.',1,2);
INSERT INTO "Question" VALUES('cmuh9bwse03lgedanh2tdeza2','cmuh9bwsd03laedanptmprhap',NULL,'MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which piece of evidence from "The Relay" best supports a theme about prioritizing the team over personal glory?','["Jordan remembers hurting the team last year by chasing the hero slot, then accepts the starting leg.","The story mentions spring weather.","Coach owns a stopwatch.","Maya''s name is easier to pronounce than Jordan''s."]',0,'The memory-plus-decision shows growth toward team-first values; weather and names are irrelevant.',1,3);
INSERT INTO "Question" VALUES('cmuh9bwsf03liedan53yn3rzw','cmuh9bwsd03laedanptmprhap',NULL,'MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Why is "Teamwork is magical" a weak theme statement for "The Relay"?','["It is a vague slogan that ignores Jordan''s specific conflict and the cost of giving up the spotlight.","It uses too many syllables for Grade 7.","Themes may never mention people or feelings.","It is weak because stories cannot be about teamwork."]',0,'Weak themes are fuzzy cheerleading; strong ones reflect the story''s particular tension and evidence.',1,4);
INSERT INTO "Question" VALUES('cmuh9bwsf03lkedanpce7mdln','cmuh9bwsd03laedanptmprhap',NULL,'MULTIPLE_CHOICE','In "Locker 214," Amira draws anonymous comics and later greets Devon in the hallway. Which theme is best supported?','["Cafeteria pizza is unhealthy, so students should never joke about food.","Amira should never sign her work if she wants to be a real artist.","Hidden kindness can start a connection, but friendship grows when someone risks being known.","Lockers are made of metal."]',2,'The move from anonymous gifts to a signed, shared comic supports a theme about risking visibility.',1,5);
INSERT INTO "Question" VALUES('cmuh9bwsf03lmedanwu66gjjd','cmuh9bwsd03laedanptmprhap',NULL,'MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A classmate says the theme of any story is whatever lesson makes readers feel good. What is the best critique?','["Theme is only found in poems, never in short fiction.","Readers should ignore characters'' choices when naming theme.","If a theme is not a fortune-cookie moral, the story has no theme.","Theme must be grounded in textual evidence; some stories develop cautionary or complicated ideas, not only cheerful morals."]',3,'Theme is evidence-based and need not be a feel-good rule.',1,6);
INSERT INTO "Question" VALUES('cmuh9bwsh03lqedanr7lr9ell','cmuh9bwsg03loedanpivbmdrq',NULL,'MULTIPLE_CHOICE','When working on “Claim, Evidence, and Reasoning,” which understanding should guide your first steps?','["Treat a counterexample as if it were the main rule.","A claim is a debatable statement you will prove — not a topic label and not a quotation by itself.","Reverse the relationship taught in the worked example.","Use an answer that fits a different lesson title entirely."]',1,'From the teach section: A claim is a debatable statement you will prove — not a topic label and not a quotation by itself.',1,1);
INSERT INTO "Question" VALUES('cmuh9bwsh03lsedanqeqafi9k','cmuh9bwsg03loedanpivbmdrq',NULL,'MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which statement correctly captures a second core idea students must use for “Claim, Evidence, and Reasoning”?','["Reverse the relationship taught in the worked example.","Use an answer that fits a different lesson title entirely.","Treat a counterexample as if it were the main rule.","Evidence is concrete: quotations, data, or specific details. Reasoning explains *how* the evidence supports the claim (use because/therefor…"]',3,'Evidence is concrete: quotations, data, or specific details. Reasoning explains *how* the evidence supports the claim (use because/therefore language).',1,2);
INSERT INTO "Question" VALUES('cmuh9bwsi03luedanjlp7iulk','cmuh9bwsg03loedanpivbmdrq',NULL,'MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A classmate rushes and invents a shortcut that contradicts “Claim, Evidence, and Reasoning.” Which reminder is accurate?','["Reverse the relationship taught in the worked example.","Weak CER skips reasoning (\"The quote proves it\") or stacks unrelated facts. Strong CER selects the best evidence and connects each piece wi…","Skip checking units, evidence, or stated constraints.","A claim is a debatable statement you will prove — not a topic label and not a quotation by itself."]',1,'Keep this idea in view: Weak CER skips reasoning ("The quote proves it") or stacks unrelated facts. Strong CER selects the best evidence and connects each piece with analysis.',1,3);
INSERT INTO "Question" VALUES('cmuh9bwsi03lwedanvymzm2or','cmuh9bwsg03loedanpivbmdrq',NULL,'MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which part of CER answers "So what?" after a quotation?','["Skip checking units, evidence, or stated constraints.","Add specific benefits (shade, play space, gatherings) and explain how each benefit supports the claim.","Reasoning — it explains how the evidence supports the claim.","Ignore definitions and choose a slogan that sounds related."]',2,'Correct response: Reasoning — it explains how the evidence supports the claim.',1,4);
INSERT INTO "Question" VALUES('cmuh9bwsj03lyedanxe4n7m3f','cmuh9bwsg03loedanpivbmdrq',NULL,'MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Fix: "Claim: Parks help towns. Evidence: Parks exist. Reasoning: Because parks exist."','["No — it is a topic. A claim would assert something about friendship.","Add specific benefits (shade, play space, gatherings) and explain how each benefit supports the claim.","Use an answer that fits a different lesson title entirely.","Treat a counterexample as if it were the main rule."]',1,'Correct response: Add specific benefits (shade, play space, gatherings) and explain how each benefit supports the claim.',1,5);
INSERT INTO "Question" VALUES('cmuh9bwsj03m0edanj9m7obj5','cmuh9bwsg03loedanpivbmdrq',NULL,'MULTIPLE_CHOICE','After missing a practice item on “Claim, Evidence, and Reasoning,” what is the most effective next move?','["Only reread the title and stop.","Skip checking units, evidence, or definitions after a miss.","Re-work the example, then explain a miss with one sentence naming the broken step.","Skip checking units, evidence, or stated constraints."]',2,'Error analysis tied to the worked example builds transferable skill — guessing does not.',1,6);
INSERT INTO "Question" VALUES('cmuh9bwsk03m4edan1nu2myns','cmuh9bwsk03m2edanpklcsidz',NULL,'MULTIPLE_CHOICE','When working on “Author''s Purpose and Tone,” which understanding should guide your first steps?','["Use an answer that fits a different lesson title entirely.","Purpose is why the author wrote: to inform, persuade, entertain, or a blend. Tone is the author''s attitude toward the subject (urgent, sarc…","Diction, imagery, and organization are clues. Loaded words signal persuasion; neutral definitions and data often signal…","Reverse the relationship taught in the worked example."]',1,'From the teach section: Purpose is why the author wrote: to inform, persuade, entertain, or a blend. Tone is the author''s attitude toward the subject (urgent, sarcastic, respectful, alarmed).',1,1);
INSERT INTO "Question" VALUES('cmuh9bwsk03m6edanaga7sxi6','cmuh9bwsk03m2edanpklcsidz',NULL,'MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which statement correctly captures a second core idea students must use for “Author''s Purpose and Tone”?','["Treat a counterexample as if it were the main rule.","Skip vocabulary and guess from the answer choices alone.","Reverse the relationship taught in the worked example.","Diction, imagery, and organization are clues. Loaded words signal persuasion; neutral definitions and data often signal informing."]',3,'Diction, imagery, and organization are clues. Loaded words signal persuasion; neutral definitions and data often signal informing.',1,2);
INSERT INTO "Question" VALUES('cmuh9bwsl03m8edanecelpquh','cmuh9bwsk03m2edanpklcsidz',NULL,'MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A classmate rushes and invents a shortcut that contradicts “Author''s Purpose and Tone.” Which reminder is accurate?','["Treat a counterexample as if it were the main rule.","Rhetorical appeals (ethos, pathos, logos) help explain *how* a speaker builds trust, emotion, or logic — always tie appeals back to specifi…","Reverse the relationship taught in the worked example.","Use an answer that fits a different lesson title entirely."]',1,'Keep this idea in view: Rhetorical appeals (ethos, pathos, logos) help explain *how* a speaker builds trust, emotion, or logic — always tie appeals back to specific lines.',1,3);
