INSERT INTO "Question" VALUES('cmuh9bx2y04otedanejorzekw','cmuh9bx2x04oredanc9na2m2s',NULL,'MULTIPLE_CHOICE','When working on “Poetry Analysis: Imagery and Sound,” which understanding should guide your first steps?','["Deny the teaching point: Simile compares using like/as; metaphor says one thing *is* another. Both create images that c…","Memorize the lesson title only and skip examples.","Simile compares using like/as; metaphor says one thing *is* another. Both create images that carry meaning beyond the literal.","Skip checking units, evidence, or stated constraints."]',2,'From the teach section: Simile compares using like/as; metaphor says one thing *is* another. Both create images that carry meaning beyond the literal.',1,1);
INSERT INTO "Question" VALUES('cmuh9bx2y04ovedanq49t63h0','cmuh9bx2x04oredanc9na2m2s',NULL,'MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which statement correctly captures a second core idea students must use for “Poetry Analysis: Imagery and Sound”?','["Ask: What two things are compared? What quality transfers? How does that shape mood or theme?","Ignore definitions and choose a slogan that sounds related.","Skip checking units, evidence, or stated constraints.","A common error is to ignore this: Ask: What two things are compared? What quality transfers? How does that shape mood o…"]',0,'Ask: What two things are compared? What quality transfers? How does that shape mood or theme?',1,2);
INSERT INTO "Question" VALUES('cmuh9bx2z04oxedanwqlz4k4u','cmuh9bx2x04oredanc9na2m2s',NULL,'MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A classmate rushes and invents a shortcut that contradicts “Poetry Analysis: Imagery and Sound.” Which reminder is accurate?','["Ask: What two things are compared? What quality transfers? How does that shape mood or theme?","Ignore definitions and choose a slogan that sounds related.","Sound devices (alliteration, repetition) and concrete sensory imagery work with figurative language to make ideas memorable.","Treat a counterexample as if it were the main rule."]',2,'Keep this idea in view: Sound devices (alliteration, repetition) and concrete sensory imagery work with figurative language to make ideas memorable.',1,3);
INSERT INTO "Question" VALUES('cmuh9bx2z04ozedan1stn3rqy','cmuh9bx2x04oredanc9na2m2s',NULL,'MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Label: "Her smile was like sunrise."','["Do the opposite of the worked example without checking.","Simile (like) — compares smile to sunrise, suggesting warmth/hope.","Treat a counterexample as if it were the main rule.","Over-literal reading, or inventing meanings with no textual support."]',1,'Correct response: Simile (like) — compares smile to sunrise, suggesting warmth/hope.',1,4);
INSERT INTO "Question" VALUES('cmuh9bx3004p1edanaliiocsp','cmuh9bx2x04oredanc9na2m2s',NULL,'MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Why might an author prefer metaphor over a plain adjective?','["To pack imagery and emotion into a compact comparison readers can picture.","Simile (like) — compares smile to sunrise, suggesting warmth/hope.","There is not enough information — so invent facts not in the lesson.","Drop all Language Arts vocabulary and pick the longest choice."]',0,'Correct response: To pack imagery and emotion into a compact comparison readers can picture.',1,5);
