INSERT INTO "Question" VALUES('cmuh9bx3d04qnedandtzp9450',NULL,'cmuh9bx3b04qbedanseic5kfy','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','[Claim, Evidence, and Reasoning] A classmate rushes and invents a shortcut that contradicts “Claim, Evidence, and Reasoning.” Which reminder is accurate?','["Reverse the relationship taught in the worked example.","Weak CER skips reasoning (\"The quote proves it\") or stacks unrelated facts. Strong CER selects the best evidence and connects each piece wi…","Skip checking units, evidence, or stated constraints.","A claim is a debatable statement you will prove — not a topic label and not a quotation by itself."]',1,'Keep this idea in view: Weak CER skips reasoning ("The quote proves it") or stacks unrelated facts. Strong CER selects the best evidence and connects each piece with analysis.',1,6);
INSERT INTO "Question" VALUES('cmuh9bx3e04qpedandmifrvtb',NULL,'cmuh9bx3b04qbedanseic5kfy','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','[Author''s Purpose and Tone] When working on “Author''s Purpose and Tone,” which understanding should guide your first steps?','["Use an answer that fits a different lesson title entirely.","Purpose is why the author wrote: to inform, persuade, entertain, or a blend. Tone is the author''s attitude toward the subject (urgent, sarc…","Diction, imagery, and organization are clues. Loaded words signal persuasion; neutral definitions and data often signal…","Reverse the relationship taught in the worked example."]',1,'From the teach section: Purpose is why the author wrote: to inform, persuade, entertain, or a blend. Tone is the author''s attitude toward the subject (urgent, sarcastic, respectful, alarmed).',1,7);
